        "description": "<p><span>\n\nWhen you have a lot of browser tabs open it can be very hard to tell\nthem apart, especially if they're from the same site:\n\n</span>\n\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.jefftk.com/top-tabs-chrome-big.png\"><img src=\"https://www.jefftk.com/top-tabs-chrome.png\" width=\"550\" height=\"361\" class=\"mobile-fullwidth\" style=\"max-width:100.0vw; max-height:65.6vw;\" srcset=\"https://www.jefftk.com/top-tabs-chrome.png 550w,https://www.jefftk.com/top-tabs-chrome-2x.png 1100w\"><div style=\"height:min(65.6vw, 361px)\" class=\"image-vertical-spacer\"></div></a>\n\n</p>\n\n<p>\n\nSince monitors are wide and websites are tall, we should very clearly\nput tabs on the side in a vertical stack.  I was a heavy user of these\nin Firefox, and when I switched to Chrome I used their <a href=\"https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40352308\">experimental</a>\n<code>--enable-vertical-tabs</code> flag.  When they <a href=\"https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40640156\">removed it</a> I\nwrote a <a href=\"https://www.jefftk.com/p/side-tabs",
